Bash	D=<craft>/skills/github; bash $D/scripts/collect.sh filing accounts/signup.py sign_up email 2>&1 | tail -5; gh repo view acme/shop --json visibility --jq .visibility 2>&1; gh label list 2>&1 | head
Bash	cat <tmp>/collect.zJ1rt3
Bash	f=$(mktemp) && cat > $f <<'EOF'; `accounts/signup.py:4`: `sign_up` checks and stores the email exactly as typed. Two emails that differ only in letter case sign up as two accounts.; ; Reproduction:; ; ```python; from accounts.signup import sign_up; sign_up("Bob@shop.test", "h"); sign_up("bob@shop.test", "h")  # expected ValueError("this email has an account"); it returns a second account; ```; ; A fix must normalize the email (for example `strip().lower()`) before the lookup and before the store, with a test that signs up `Bob@shop.test` and then `bob@shop.test`. Decide if the account keeps the email as typed for display.; ; Out of scope here: found in the review of the branch for #14, which changes only `orders/`.; EOF; gh issue create --title "Sign-up treats emails that differ only in case as different accounts" --label bug --body-file $f
Bash	python3 -m pytest -q 2>&1 | tail -3; git status --short
Bash	rm -rf accounts/__pycache__ billing/__pycache__ && git status --short
text	I checked all three findings against the code. None of them went into this branch, because all three are outside `orders/`, the module of #14. I changed no code, and the branch and the working tree are clean.  1. **`get_invoice_total` name (nit):** I filed no issue and changed nothing. It is a naming preference, and no project rule asks for it. 2. **`vat` docstring (nit):** The finding is correct. `vat(Decimal("10.00"))` returns `2.00`, which is euros, not cents. The project's rules count a wrong comment as a nit, so I filed no issue. The fix is to change "in cents" to "in euros" in `billing/tax.py:4`. You can file it or fix it yourself. 3. **Sign-up email case (defect):** The finding is correct. `Bob@shop.test` and then `bob@shop.test` make two accounts. No issue in the tracker covered it, so I filed **#17** with the label `bug`. It has the steps to reproduce, and notes that a fix needs a test and a choice about whether to keep the email as typed for display.  **Check:** I could not run the tests. `python -m pytest` fails because pytest is not installed here, so no tests ran.  **Docs impact:** none, because no code changed.
